C0 drill — S3 (LocalStack) + KMS envelope + SSE-KMS, key-cached
2026-08-09 · rig: bench/compose/timelorddb-s3.yml (LocalStack 4.9,
s3+kms; init hook creates alias/timelord + buckets with default
SSE-KMS + BucketKeyEnabled). Ports 3966/3967. What this rig may claim:
correctness, call counts, recovery. NOT latency.
================================================================================

Stack under test: EncryptingStore(CachingKms(AwsKms), S3Store) — every
object envelope-encrypted client-side AND SSE-KMS'd server-side, WAL on
a local volume, everything else in the bucket (CL-1).

1. THE BUG THE RIG CAUGHT FIRST (fixed before the gate, run
   c0-s3-cache-on-20260809-093521 records it): acked rows went
   unqueryable during a table's FIRST flush — buffer swapped out before
   the catalog commit, and S3 upload latency stretched that
   microsecond-wide local window to ~100s of ms. Intermittent
   "table 'poc.public.pipeline_events' not found" hit B1/B2 (3 errors).
   Fix: rows mid-flush stay served from a holding snapshot until the
   catalog commit lands; readers consult holding BEFORE the catalog so
   the residual micro-window errs toward a transient duplicate (the
   documented M2 tolerance), never a vanish. Regression-pinned by
   rows_stay_visible_while_a_slow_flush_uploads (150 ms/put SlowStore,
   probes across the whole window).

2. Smoke gate on S3, cache ON (run c0-s3-cache-on2-20260809-094702):
   72,806 + 24,000 lines, 0 errors; Shape A errors=0; Shape B
   all_completed=True; burst 5,000 clean with concurrent query ok;
   context counts EXACT (77,806 / 3,000 / 21,000).

3. KMS key cache, measured (identical smoke workload, 56 object PUTs
   = 55 parquet + 1 manifest):
                          cache ON        cache OFF
   GenerateDataKey        1               56
   engine-side hits       55              (no cache)
   Decrypt                0               0
   LocalStack's own API log confirms both counts independently.
   56:1 on 56 objects; the window is 300 s / 1,000 uses, so at full
   scale "thousands of calls" stays a handful per window. Cache-off
   read-path corollary: recovery + one full-table scan cost 54 Decrypts
   (one per distinct object header) — the decrypt LRU makes repeats
   free.

4. At rest, both layers on one object
   (objects/poc/disk_metrics/data/.../...parquet):
   head-object: SSE=aws:kms, KMS key=alias/timelord, BucketKeyEnabled=true
   get-object bytes: T L D E 1  (client envelope; no PAR1 exposed)

5. CAS fidelity (ARCHITECTURE §16 risk 6 — RETIRED): in-network
   integration tests (cargo test -p timelord-store-s3 -- --ignored)
   pass: full Store contract over S3 including range semantics at EOF,
   directory-scoped list, and put_if_absent — LocalStack 4.9 honors
   If-None-Match: * (winner wins, loser gets 412 → Ok(false)). The C1
   two-writer catalog drill can run on this rig. Also green: AwsKms
   generate/unwrap roundtrip and the full encrypted stack over S3
   (20 objects, 1 GenerateDataKey, 0 Decrypts).

6. Restart drill: docker restart -t 0 → recovery replays 55 files from
   KMS-encrypted S3 manifests, COUNT(*) = 77,806 exact.

Environment note: localstack/localstack:latest is license-gated as of
the 2026 rebrand and exits(55) without a token; the rig pins
localstack/localstack:4.9 (community, s3+kms, conditional writes).

Workspace gate: cargo test --workspace green (16 server integration
tests incl. the new slow-flush probe; store 14; store-s3 1 unit + 2
LocalStack-gated), clippy 0 warnings, fmt clean.
